{
  "schema": "semio.framework.tool-machine.gesture-drive-law.v1",
  "note": "🌊️ The shared streamed-gesture runner (design §5 of ticket 26/09/30/NON-DESTRUCTIVE-HISTORY-EDITING): one dispatch of a window's streamed tool (a gumball drag, a paint stroke) from the gesture its window persisted. `phases` pins the wire `phase`/`reason` arguments (absent phase = one-shot, absent reason = `tool`, unknown words refused). Every row drives the counting `tool` from `persisted` through `dispatch` in Rust (`drive_gesture`) and TypeScript (`driveGesture`) and must report `expected`: the reason the open gesture was aborted with, the ticks committed as ONE transaction, the window's next gesture and the refusal that faulted the dispatch. A gesture its tool cannot restore is dropped with zero trace and the dispatch runs from rest; a refused start or tick has no effect at all. Authored by `🧪️s4-tools-a-gesture-drive-law.py`, an independent Python model.",
  "tool": {
    "refuseStartVerb": "refuse-start",
    "startRefusal": "toolTransaction.closed",
